  "prompt_text": "The figure uses Bloch spheres to illustrate three basic quantum ideas for a qubit. In the “two-state system” view, the qubit is shown in one of two definite basis states, represented by arrows pointing straight up or straight down along the z-axis, corresponding to \\(|0\\rangle\\) and \\(|1\\rangle\\). This emphasizes that a simple measurement basis has two discrete outcomes.\n\nThe “superposition” part contrasts this with a state whose arrow points in an intermediate direction rather than exactly along the axis. That slanted vector indicates the qubit is not purely \\(|0\\rangle\\) or \\(|1\\rangle\\), but a combination of both, with its orientation on the sphere encoding the state.\n\nOn the right, the “entanglement” section shows two qubits with linked, opposite-looking states connected by a double arrow. Each qubit individually appears in an intermediate state before measurement, but once one is measured, the result collapses to a definite pole and the partner correspondingly ends up in the matching correlated outcome. The visual message is that superposition describes uncertainty within one qubit, while entanglement describes strong measurement correlations between two qubits.",
